<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Acrocorinth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Acrocorinth is the steep rock, about 575 metres high, that rises above ancient Corinth in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It served as the city's acropolis, with a temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> on its summit, and as one of the strongest fortresses in Greece, controlling the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a>. Held in turn by Macedonians, the Achaean League, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, Venetians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, it preserves walls from many periods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/acrocorinth.jpg" alt="Acrocorinth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: elveoflight · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akrokorinth_nordmauern.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Acrocorinth, in Greek the acropolis of ancient Corinth, is a monolithic rock overlooking the ancient city. George Forrest judged it the most impressive acropolis of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. It has a secure water supply, and its fortress was repeatedly used as a last line of defence in southern Greece because it commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-corinth">Isthmus of Corinth</a> and kept foes from entering the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> by land. The Upper Pirene spring lies within the encircling walls. According to a Corinthian myth told by Pausanias in the 2nd century CE, the giant Briareus judged between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helios">Helios</a>, awarding the Isthmus to Poseidon and Acrocorinth to Helios.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The rock was continuously occupied from archaic times to the early 19th century. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it formed, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a>, one of the three so-called Fetters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, fortresses garrisoned by the Macedonians to secure their control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a>. The archaic acropolis was already easy to defend because of its shape. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> fortified it heavily when it became the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a> of the thema of Hellas and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>. Leo Sgouros defended it against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> for three years. Afterwards it was a fortress of the Frankish Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a>, the Venetians and the Ottoman Turks.</p><p>Three circuit walls formed the man-made defence of the hill. The highest peak held a temple to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a>, which was converted into a church and then into a mosque. The American School's Corinth Excavations began work on Acrocorinth in 1929, and it is now one of the most important medieval castle sites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Acrocorinth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 243 BCE Aratus captured <b>Acrocorinth</b> from its Macedonian garrison in a night assault, and Corinth, Megara, Troezen and Epidaurus joined.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaean-league">Achaean League · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Leo Sgouros held <b>Acrocorinth</b> until his death in 1208, Corinth fell in 1210 and Argos and Nauplia in 1212, while Venice kept the ports of Modon and Coron in the south-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Corinth grew in the early Iron Age on the Isthmus linking the Peloponnese to central Greece, below the fortress hill of <b>Acrocorinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a></li><li><blockquote>He tried to block Boniface of Montferrat at Thermopylae in 1204 but retreated to the near-impregnable citadel of <b>Acrocorinth</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-leo-sgouros">Lordship of Leo Sgouros</a></li></ul></section>
